<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/advanced/coordination-chemistry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Transition metals do something no other family can: they hold several neighbours at once in
    a structured embrace. A central metal ion collects <strong>ligands</strong> — water, ammonia,
    cyanide, haem's ring — into geometric complexes whose properties (colour, magnetism,
    reactivity) can be tuned almost continuously. This is the chemistry of blood, catalysts,
    gems and the vitamin B₁₂ in your breakfast.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the field-split story — why the same metal can wear many colours" widget="equationStep" config={coordCfg} />

  <h2>Why complexes colour-shift</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is blue; add ammonia and [Cu(NH₃)₆]²⁺ turns deep violet-blue</strong> — same metal, new ligands, different d-orbital split. The same physics paints ruby red and emerald green (chromium in different surroundings).</li>
    <li><strong>Catalysis is coordination's day job:</strong> catalytic converters, the Haber process (iron surface), Ziegler–Natta plastic catalysts (Nobel 1963) and enzyme mimics — metals hold reactants in just the right geometry to react.</li>
    <li><strong>Chelation:</strong> claw-like ligands grip a metal at several points at once. EDTA chelates lead in lead poisoning; haemoglobin's chelated iron carries oxygen — and carbon monoxide binds it 200× more tightly, which is why CO kills.</li>
    <li><strong>Cis–platin:</strong> a square-planar platinum complex that crosslinks cancer-cell DNA. Geometry as medicine — Nobel 1973 for the structure type, and a mainstay of chemotherapy since 1978.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Werner's coordination theory (1893); OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Transition Metals); Nobel Prize citations 1963/1973.</p>
</TopicPage>
